0.0189361572265625, 0.021270751953125, 0.035675048828125, -0.00818634033203125, -0.005992889404296875, -0.006748199462890625, 0.02301025390625, 0.0007843971252441406, -0.01068878173828125, 0.048919677734375, -0.0251617431640625, 0.0175933837890625, -0.00653839111328125, 0.01303863525390625, 0.0197601318359375, 0.017364501953125, 0.0355224609375, -0.02008056640625, 0.041046142578125, 0.0088348388671875, 0.034271240234375, -0.006771087646484375, -0.0009255409240722656, 0.0005559921264648438, -0.013519287109375, 0.007579803466796875, -8.338689804077148e-05, -0.00844573974609375, 0.0193634033203125, 0.053070068359375, -0.0138092041015625, -0.01522064208984375, -0.03497314453125, 0.029571533203125, -0.0121612548828125, -0.005107879638671875, 0.03961181640625, 0.00893402099609375, 0.0021839141845703125, -0.0227813720703125, 0.004230499267578125, -0.00018072128295898438, -0.00609588623046875, -0.02642822265625, 0.01480865478515625, -0.030364990234375, -0.01412200927734375, -0.004337310791015625, -0.0072784423828125, 0.02813720703125, 0.0096588134765625, 0.00033211708068847656, -0.01154327392578125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Large-scale image generation has advanced through improvements in data scale, quality, rebalancing, and recaptioning. However, conventional pipelines often optimize task-specific datasets in isolation, leading to fragmented supervision and limited transfer across generative tasks. For example, text-to-image (T2I) datasets may lack the editing annotations needed for image manipulation, while editing corpora are rarely aligned with structured knowledge sources. This siloed approach makes it challenging to systematically organize heterogeneous supervision and to leverage dependencies among generative capabilities.\n\nTo address these limitations, we introduce a \\textbf{capability-driven data infrastructure}: a unified framework that explicitly organizes and schedules data collection and curation according to the progression of model capabilities. This infrastructure is designed to support scalable, curriculum-aligned training for multimodal generative models. At its core, the framework comprises three interoperable data engines, each responsible for a distinct type of \\textbf{capability-specific supervision}\u2014that is, tailored data and annotations for training models on specific generative tasks:\n\n(1) \\textit{Text-image grounding}: This engine pairs descriptive captions with images to enable T2I generation (e.g., matching \"a red sports car on a mountain road\" to its corresponding image).  \n(2) \\textit{Inter-image transformation}: This engine creates editing pairs by associating an original image with its modified version and the corresponding edit instruction (e.g., turning a daytime scene into nighttime based on a prompt).  \n(3) \\textit{Image-knowledge association}: This engine links images to structured knowledge entities (e.g., connecting an image of the Eiffel Tower to its Wikidata entity).\n\nThese engines are interoperable: for instance, an image and its caption from the grounding engine can be used as a base for generating editing pairs, while knowledge associations can enrich both T2I and editing data with semantic context. This interoperability enables the construction of multi-faceted datasets that support transfer and compositionality across tasks.\n\nThe curriculum scheduling process unfolds in a logically connected sequence:  \n- First, we introduce simpler capabilities (e.g., basic T2I grounding) before more complex ones (e.g., editing or knowledge association), ensuring foundational skills are established.  \n- Next, we adjust the visual-concept distribution to achieve broad and balanced coverage, building on the initial capability foundation.  \n- Then, we progressively enhance data quality and image resolution, leveraging the established concept diversity.  \n- Finally, we follow the dependency order of capability acquisition, so that advanced skills are layered atop robust foundational abilities. For example, editing tasks are introduced after the model demonstrates proficiency in T2I generation, ensuring a smooth progression.\n\nTo align T2I and editing supervision across tasks and granularities, we employ \\textbf{caption experts}: automated captioning models that are fine-tuned for accuracy and consistency using a held-out validation set and human-in-the-loop feedback. Caption experts are selected based on their performance on benchmark captioning tasks and are periodically evaluated for coherence and relevance by expert annotators, ensuring high-quality supervision across the data engines.\n\nAt scale, our framework curates a 440M-image T2I corpus, 120M editing pairs, and over 27M image-entity pairs. For context, this surpasses the size and diversity of many existing datasets: for example, LAION-5B contains 400M English-language image-text pairs, while OpenImages offers 9M images with limited editing or knowledge annotations. Our datasets exhibit higher diversity, as measured by unique object and scene categories, and broader coverage of compositional and knowledge-grounded scenarios.\n\nUsing this infrastructure, we train multimodal diffusion models from scratch at two scales, with 3B and 6B parameters respectively. For evaluation, we conduct quantitative assessment on CPI-Bench\u2014a comprehensive benchmark designed to test compositional, perceptual, and instruction-following abilities in generative models\u2014using metrics such as FID, CLIP score, and instruction adherence. Qualitative evaluations include reviews by a panel of 15 human and expert annotators, selected for domain expertise and diversity, with inter-rater reliability measured at 0.87 (Cohen\u2019s kappa) across diverse T2I and editing scenarios.\n\nExperimental results demonstrate broad visual coverage, versatile rendering, and effective transfer across generative capabilities. In summary, our capability-driven data infrastructure enables scalable, curriculum-aligned training for multimodal generative models, setting a new standard for dataset construction and model performance in large-scale image generation. Potential limitations include the computational cost of large-scale curation and the need for further refinement of capability dependencies; future work will explore more efficient data selection and broader capability hierarchies."}}